a perilous path to poland  is only the beginning  refugees from the war in ukraine keep  going  exhausted  they begin another difficult journey  many board trains leaving eastern poland  not knowing where their last stop will  be  one woman overwhelmed tired barely able  to speak  said she left her whole family back in  ukraine  her husband is fighting in the war  children are caring for her elderly  mother who's too old to leave  carrying what little she can she's  heading west hoping to find work  perhaps a new home for her family  when we met her on the train  she said she had not slept in four days  is going back to her job in warsaw after  volunteering at the border she was  mostly greeting refugees for example  yesterday i saw a woman with her  grandson and she was  crying so i said  upon to her and we were talking a little  bit i don't know ukrainian language but  it's similar to polish language so she  was talking about her family  her husband her home that she  has left now  so the support emotional support is  something that i think they need the  most  if the war continues she says more  volunteers will need to go to the border  something as simple as a warm welcome at  poland's door  can make those forced out of their  country  feel more at home  [Applause]  two years old tamur is too young to  understand that he's been uprooted by  war  [Music]  his father is fighting russian forces in  donbass  fleeing the violence his mother says was  their only choice  what can people do now they can only run  with their children we were hesitating  for a long time about what we should do  but i think it was the right decision  every train going west is filled  with stories of lives torn apart by war  millions of vulnerable civilians forced  to find a fresh start  once the train pulls into krakow station  people step off the platform into a kind  of controlled chaos now it is hectic  here but there is help to be had there's  volunteers handing out food  available to answer any questions people  might have and helping people register  to find a place to live  on the train some do find their way  overhearing her story agnieszka far from  the border but still helping  offers a ride and a place to stay to a  new friend  safe passage and safe haven  new beginnings in krakow  a city that has known war  now a place of refuge  and resistance  not the closest kind zayn bastravi  al-jazeera krakow
 

